{-
   What `@heap` refuses - the attribute side of §10.4's placement ladder.

   # resolver: ownership

   `@heap` moves one binding's object off the frame and is the only thing in the language that
   selects a rung directly, so the three ways of writing it wrongly are worth a fixture of their own.
   The messages are this stage's; the shipping stage refuses the same programs in its own words,
   which is why this is marked rather than shared.

   A binding that names storage this frame does not own has no object to place, and saying so beats
   minting one for the attribute to be about - which is also why `let @heap q = p` at a `TrivialCopy`
   type is *accepted*: that binding takes a copy, and a copy is an object of its own. An attribute a
   binding does not take is **reported** rather than dropped, which is the half this position did not
   have at all: `VarDecl::attributes` was parsed and thrown away, so a misspelling compiled and did
   nothing.

   What is **not** here is the escape half. A value that has to outlive its frame is refused where
   the escape is written rather than by this attribute - `generic/Reject.ClosureEscape.yana`.
-}

data Pair {left: Int, right: Int}

data Guard {id: Int}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->self: Guard):
        {}

fn borrowed(g: Guard) -> Int:
    -- A second name for the caller's value, so there is nothing here to place.
    let @heap q = g
    return q.id

fn arguments() -> Int:
    let @heap(2) &p = Pair {left: 1, right: 2}
    return p.left

fn unknown() -> Int:
    let @hep &p = Pair {left: 1, right: 2}
    return p.left

fn main() -> Int = borrowed(Guard {id: 1}) + arguments() + unknown()
